window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] = window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] || {};
window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][141] = "<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> interview-craft · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C33 · <strong>Competency:</strong> IC · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> You have loops at two companies in the same month, one built on stories and a written exercise, the other on product sense and analytical rounds. How do you prepare?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I'd study as much as I can for both.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Identifies the differences and allocates time.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Separates shared from specific preparation with hours, confirms formats, allocates by importance times gap, schedules the loops, plans mocks, and keeps policies and briefs separate.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;First I'd confirm each format with the recruiter: round types, durations, tools, decider and the policy on assistance. Then I'd split the work into what is shared and what is specific. Shared: the story bank with probes, metrics fundamentals, structure and communication, product sense basics. Specific: for the stories-and-written-exercise company, written practice and probe depth; for the other, timed product and analytical rounds in 45-minute format and committee-style evidence. &quot;With about 50 hours each if prepared separately, 100 in all, suppose the shared work is 24 hours and each specific part 26: 24 + 26 + 26 = 76 hours, a saving of a quarter. I'd allocate within each part by importance times gap: after a mock for each, I'd rate my strength by round type and put hours where importance is high and strength low (as in Worked example 1). &quot;The schedule: weeks one and two, the shared work and two mocks; week three, the first company's specific work and its loop; week four, the second company's specific work and its loop. I'd write a separate one-page company brief for each and keep their policies straight (one may prohibit assistance explicitly). After the first loop I'd write down what I was asked, re-score and adjust the second plan. I'd stop new material two days before each loop, and plan logistics, including time zones, for both.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Format first; shared versus specific with numbers; weights; schedule; learning between loops.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c34-01\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C34-01</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> interview-craft · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C34 · <strong>Competency:</strong> IC · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> How would you structure your practice for the month before a loop?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I'd practice as many questions as I can.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> A schedule of mocks, review and a taper.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Timed partner mocks with scorecards, calibration, an error log with one fix per session and retests, a data-driven target from the weakest dimension, and a protected final week.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;I'd assume about eight hours a week and spend it on retrieval, not rereading. Week one: build the loop map with the recruiter (C33.1), read only to learn methods, and run three solo timed drills and one partner mock, scoring myself first with the scorecard and then comparing with the partner. Week two: two partner mocks, recorded, with an error log and one fix per session, retested within 72 hours; compute my bias against the partner. Week three: diagnose my weakest important dimension from the scores and put about half of my hours there with a numeric goal; one full mock loop at the end. Week four: taper. A last partner mock of one round type on day minus three, light review of story headlines and the company brief, logistics settled two days before, and rest the day before. I'd judge progress by calibrated scores by dimension on realistic mocks, not by hours logged.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Retrieval-based; scorecards; calibration; a data-driven target; a taper.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c34-02\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C34-02</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> interview-craft · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C34 · <strong>Competency:</strong> IC · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> How do you give useful feedback to a peer after a mock interview?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I tell them what I liked and what to improve.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Scores against a rubric with evidence and gives one priority fix.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Scores independently before discussing, quotes the evidence, separates strengths from fixes, keeps to one or two fixes, asks the peer to self-score first, and agrees a retest.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;Before the mock I ask the peer what rubric we are using and what they want me to probe. During it I take notes as evidence statements, quoting what they said. At the end we each score independently before talking. Then I ask them to share their score first and the evidence for it, which makes them self-assess, and I compare: where we differ by two points or more, we look at the quote and discuss who had the better evidence. &quot;I give two strengths with evidence ('you named the user and the number in the first minute') and one or two fixes, not ten: 'You gave a metric without a denominator; try stating numerator and denominator.' I avoid dumping solutions: I say what I observed and the effect, and let them choose how to fix it. We agree one fix and a retest on a different prompt within three days. I also thank them, since honest feedback is uncomfortable to receive and easy to soften.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Independent scoring; evidence; priorities; a retest.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c34-03\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C34-03</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> interview-craft · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C34 · <strong>Competency:</strong> IC · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Tell me about a time you received critical feedback.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I was told to improve my communication and I did.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names the feedback, the change and a result.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Shows openness (asked for specifics), a tested change with numbers, an outside check of the fix, a lasting habit, and honesty about the initial reaction.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> <em>(A composite story, shown as notes then told.)</em> <em>Notes.</em> Context: my first readout to senior stakeholders was a deck of 22 slides with charts and no recommendation; my manager said afterward, &quot;I didn't know what you wanted us to decide&quot; (invented). Decision: defend the work (it was thorough), or change the format. Action: I asked her for an example of a readout she liked; she sent a one-page memo with the recommendation first; I rewrote mine as a one-page decision document with the ask, options, recommendation and what would change my mind; I asked two colleagues outside the project to read it and say the point after one read; the first could not, so I cut a third and rewrote the opening. Result: the next readout took 20 minutes instead of 60, and the decision was made in the meeting; stakeholders approved the proposal in one session instead of two. Learning: the reader's time is the measure, and I now ask for feedback after each deliverable with a specific question: &quot;What would have made this easier to act on?&quot; <em>Told (about 1 minute 10 seconds).</em> &quot;After my first readout to senior stakeholders, my manager told me she didn't know what I wanted them to decide. My deck had 22 slides and no recommendation, and my first reaction was that the thoroughness showed effort. I could defend it or change it. I asked her for an example she liked, which was a one-page memo with the recommendation first, and I rewrote mine in that form: the ask, the options, my recommendation and what would change my mind. I asked two colleagues to read it and tell me the point after one read; the first couldn't, so I cut a third and rewrote the opening. The next readout took 20 minutes instead of 60 and we got the decision in the room. What I took from it is that the reader's time is the measure, and I now ask for feedback on every major piece with one question: what would have made this easier to act on?&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Honesty about the first reaction; a tested change; an outside check; a lasting habit.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c34-04\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C34-04</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> interview-craft · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C34 · <strong>Competency:</strong> IC · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> You did well in mock interviews but froze in the real one. What happened, and what would you change?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I was nervous; I'd stay calmer.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Identifies differences between mocks and the real round and plans practice.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Diagnoses transfer gaps (stakes, an unfamiliar interviewer, no pause, an unfamiliar format), plans pressure mocks and a routine with recovery phrases, uses the log, and treats the bad round as data without spiraling.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;I'd first write down what happened within an hour: which question, what I felt, what I did, and how long it lasted. Then I'd look for what differed from my mocks. Typically: stakes (a real outcome); an unfamiliar interviewer with a neutral face; a format or tool I had not used; no ability to restart; and fatigue in a later round. I'd check each against my log. &quot;Then I'd change the practice. First, make mocks resemble the real thing more: a partner I don't know, the real tool, a stranger's neutral expression, no restarts, and a full loop with breaks to practice stamina. Second, build a routine and recovery moves: a slow first sentence, the plan stated aloud, and three phrases: 'Let me take a moment to structure this,' 'I want to correct something,' and 'Let me summarize where I am.' I'd rehearse them aloud until they are automatic. Third, practice the specific moment: have the partner stop me mid-answer with a hard question and practice recovering, since the freeze often comes from an unexpected interruption. &quot;And I'd treat the bad round as one data point. I'd reset between rounds (stand, water, one line on what to improve) and not replay it. After the loop I'd write what I'd change and fix one or two things. A freeze is common; what matters is whether the next attempt shows a better recovery.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Diagnosis; realistic practice; rehearsed recovery; resilience.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c34-05\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C34-05</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> interview-craft · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C34 · <strong>Competency:</strong> IC · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> How do you know when you are ready for a loop?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;When I feel confident.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Uses mock scores and consistency.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Sets measurable criteria (calibrated scores by dimension, timing, probe survival, full loops), uses outside scorers, recognizes that feeling is unreliable, and plans the taper.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;Not by feeling, since fluency with the material isn't the same as performing under conditions (CS-C34-02). I'd set measurable criteria and check them. First, calibrated scores: an average of at least 3.5 across round types over the last six realistic mocks, with no dimension central to a round below 3. Second, consistency: the spread across mocks small, so that I'm not strong one day and weak the next. Third, timing: finishing each round type within about ten percent of its budget with the recommendation and risks included. Fourth, stories: each of my bank survives three levels of probing without invented detail, and no story repeats across rounds. Fifth, at least two full mock loops with breaks, scored by outside partners, with the last one at a similar level to the first and no sharp fade in later rounds. Sixth, logistics: the format, the policy, the tools and the time zone confirmed. &quot;If those hold, I'm as ready as practice can make me, and the remaining step is rest. If a criterion fails, that tells me what to work on and whether to move the date, which is sometimes possible and worth asking the recruiter about early. Readiness is also not perfection: no one is at a 5 on everything, and waiting for it means never interviewing.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Measurable criteria; outside scoring; consistency; honesty about feeling.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c34-06\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C34-06</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> interview-craft · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C34 · <strong>Competency:</strong> IC · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> How would you use an AI tool to prepare for interviews without fooling yourself?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I'd ask it to give me questions and feedback.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names the risk of flattery and inaccuracy.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Uses a rubric, quoted evidence, two runs, calibration against a human with a bias figure, verification of facts, a division of use (prompts and critique, not final scores), live human mocks, and a rule against use in real interviews unless permitted.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;I'd use it for what it does well and check it for what it does badly. It is good for generating prompts by round type, role-playing an interviewer with probes, summarizing my answer to show where the structure broke, and suggesting follow-ups. It is poor at scoring honestly: models tend to flatter, to be inconsistent, to prefer long fluent answers and to invent facts about companies. &quot;So I'd have a routine (FW-C34-08). I'd give it the scorecard and ask for a score with a quote from my answer for each dimension; if it can't quote me, I discard the score. I'd ask for the strongest criticism first. I'd run it twice and compare: big differences mean noise. I'd calibrate it against a human: I'd have a partner and the tool score the same five answers. Suppose the tool gives 9, 8 and 9 where the partner gives 6, 5 and 6: the tool's bias is +3. Then I'd treat its scores as meaningless and use it only for prompts and critique, or subtract the bias if it is steady. I'd verify every fact about a company or a process against primary sources. I'd keep live mocks with people at least weekly, since the tool applies no real pressure. I wouldn't paste confidential material from an employer. And I'd follow each company's policy: some say you may not use AI tools in the interview, and I'd comply.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Division of labor; evidence quotes; a bias calculation; verification; compliance.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c34-07\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C34-07</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> interview-craft · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C34 · <strong>Competency:</strong> IC · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Your self-scores are consistently higher than your partner's. What do you do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I'd try to be more critical of myself.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Computes the gap and checks the evidence.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Computes bias and error, finds the dimension-level pattern, uses anchors and quoted evidence, corrects the scores, rechecks after a few mocks and uses the partner's scores for decisions meanwhile.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;I'd measure it. Over eight mocks, suppose my scores are 4, 4, 3, 5, 4, 3, 4, 5 and my partner's are 3, 3, 3, 4, 3, 3, 3, 4. The differences are +1, +1, 0, +1, +1, 0, +1, +1: a bias of +0.75 and an error of 0.75. That's consistent, so it can be corrected: I'd treat my 4 as about a 3.25. Then I'd find why: where we differ, I'd compare the evidence. Maybe I credit myself for naming a framework while the partner scores whether it fit the problem; maybe I credit a metric for being named while they want a numerator, denominator and guardrail. That tells me what my scale lacks. I'd fix it with the written anchors (reread before scoring) and by quoting the sentence that supports each score. &quot;Meanwhile I'd use the partner's scores to choose what to practice, since mine are inflated. After three more mocks with anchors and quotes, I'd recompute: if the bias falls to about +0.3, the correction is working. If it does not, I'd get a second scorer, in case my partner is the unusual one.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Measurement; a pattern; a mechanism; a recheck.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c34-08\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C34-08</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> interview-craft · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C34 · <strong>Competency:</strong> IC · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Design a mock-interview program for a cohort of 30 aspiring product managers.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Pair them up and have them do mock interviews.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Defines users, problems and a structure with scoring.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Identifies the binding constraint (interviewer supply and feedback quality), designs pairing and swapping, scorecards and calibration sessions, uses AI for solo practice with checks, sizes the load, and measures outcomes with guardrails.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;Users: the 30 candidates (and volunteer experienced PMs as optional coaches). The problem I'd focus on: not enough good practice with reliable feedback; the binding constraint is interviewer supply and feedback quality, not motivation. Evidence I'd gather first: how many mocks each person has done, how their self-scores compare with outside scores, and which round types they lack practice in. &quot;Design. <em>Structure:</em> each candidate does two partner mocks a week, swapping roles so that everyone interviews as well as is interviewed, which solves supply: 30 candidates × 2 mocks = 60 mocks a week, each about an hour, so 60 candidate hours and 60 interviewer hours, which the swap covers. <em>Quality:</em> a shared scorecard with anchors for each round type; a short training session where everyone scores the same recorded mock and we compare, to calibrate the group; a coach (an experienced PM) joins one mock a month per pair. <em>Solo practice:</em> an AI tool for prompts and critique, with the checking routine, and a rule that live mocks come first. <em>Rotation:</em> partners rotate every two weeks, so no one is tuned to one style. <em>Data:</em> each person's calibrated scores by dimension and an error log, reviewed every two weeks; the weakest dimension for each gets targeted drills. &quot;Metrics: mean calibrated score by dimension at the start and the end; the bias of self-scores; timing adherence; the share of participants completing at least 10 scored mocks; and, over a longer period, interview progression. Guardrails: participant stress and drop-out, and the burden on volunteer coaches. I'd launch with a pilot of eight people for three weeks, check whether calibration sessions reduce bias, and expand.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> The constraint; a self-sustaining design; calibration; AI with checks; measurement.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c34-09\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C34-09</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> interview-craft · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C34 · <strong>Competency:</strong> IC · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Walk me through the final week before a loop.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I'd review everything again and practice a lot.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> A taper with light review and logistics.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> A day-by-day plan with a final full mock at day minus seven, targeted drills, no new material in the last three days, logistics settled two days before, and rest, with a day-of routine and an after protocol.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;Day minus seven: a last full mock loop, scored, and choose the one or two fixes that matter most. Minus six: drill those fixes; update the error log; confirm in writing the times, the time zone, the tools and the policy on assistance. Minus five: review the company brief and my 'why us'; say my tailored stories aloud. Minus four: two short drills on the weakest dimension; no new frameworks. Minus three: one partner mock of a single round type. Minus two: light review of story headlines, the brief and my questions; a tech check on the real device and network; stop new material. Minus one: rest: a short walk, one page of review, early sleep; prepare clothes, water, notes. Day zero: wake early, eat, light movement, a fifteen-minute review of headlines, a final tech check and a breath before each round; between rounds, reset. After: notes within an hour, a thank-you within a day, and rest. &quot;What I avoid: cramming new material, which raises anxiety and doesn't stick; a full mock the day before, which tires me; and unconfirmed logistics. If the loop is at an awkward hour in my time zone, I adjust sleep earlier in the week.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> A taper; logistics; routines; discipline about what not to do.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c34-10\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C34-10</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> interview-craft · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C34 · <strong>Competency:</strong> IC · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> How would you evaluate an AI tool that scores mock interviews before recommending it to others?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I'd try it and see if the feedback seems good.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Compares its scores with human scores.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Builds an eval: a sample of real answers scored by several experts, agreement among the experts, the tool's bias, error and consistency against their consensus, evidence quality, fairness across accents and styles, a pass bar, and a rollout with monitoring.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;I'd treat it as an AI feature and build an eval (C25). First, a set of answers: about 250, from real mocks across round types and levels, with consent. Sizing: to estimate the tool's agreement with the human consensus within ±5 points at about 80% agreement needs 1.96² × 0.8 × 0.2 ÷ 0.05² ≈ 246 cases. Second, human scores: three experienced interviewers score each answer independently with the same scorecard; I'd compute their agreement beyond chance (kappa) and fix the anchors until it is acceptable, since a tool can't be better than its reference. Third, the tool's performance: bias (mean tool minus consensus), error (mean absolute difference), the share within one point, and consistency (two runs of the same answer). Fourth, evidence: does it quote the answer for each score? I'd sample fifty and check that the quotes exist and support the score. Fifth, fairness: compare bias by accent, speaking style, language of origin and answer length, since tools can penalize or reward style. &quot;Pass bar, for example: bias within ±0.3 points, error under 0.6, 85% within one point, consistency within one point on 90% of repeated runs, evidence quotes valid in at least 95% of samples, no group with bias differing by more than 0.3. If it passes, recommend it for critique and practice scoring with the caveat to confirm with a human each week, monitor on new answers monthly, and publish its measured limits.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> A sized eval; reference reliability; bias, error and consistency; evidence validity; fairness; a pass bar.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c37-01\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C37-01</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> interview-craft · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C37 · <strong>Competency:</strong> IC · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> How do you keep up with this industry?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I read tech news and follow people on social media.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names primary sources and a routine.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Describes a weekly routine with primary sources, dated facts, an application step and a specific recent example, and says how it separates fact from inference.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;I use a one-hour weekly routine built on primary sources. Twenty minutes scanning the official newsrooms of a few companies I follow, such as Google's Keyword blog and Meta's newsroom; twenty-five minutes reading one primary document in depth, such as a shareholder letter or a filing section; and fifteen minutes applying it: who a launch is for, what I would measure, one risk and one question. For example, Netflix's shareholder letter for the third quarter of 2023 said that membership in its ad-supported plan was up almost 70% over the previous quarter and that 30% of sign-ups in markets with the plan chose it <code>[V: shareholder letter, October 2023]</code>. What I took from it was that relative growth tells me nothing without the base and that I'd want to know whether those sign-ups were new demand or customers moving down from a higher-priced plan. &quot;I keep each fact with its source and date in a note, and I label anything I can't source as my inference, so that I don't present a guess as a fact.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Primary sources; a routine; a specific example; fact versus inference.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c37-02\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C37-02</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> interview-craft · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C37 · <strong>Competency:</strong> IC · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> What would you do in your first 30 days as an associate product manager?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Meet the team and learn the product.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names what to learn: users, metrics, partners, decisions.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> A concrete plan with deliverables, a written agreement with the manager, and a one-page output by day 30; says what it would not do.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;I'd aim to learn how decisions get made and what the numbers say, not to change anything yet. In the first week I'd agree with my manager what success looks like at 30, 60 and 90 days, in writing, and set a weekly 30-minute check-in. I'd use the product as a user for several hours, read the support tickets and the last two quarters of launch reviews, and meet each partner for 30 minutes with the same questions: what do you need from a product manager, what has gone well and badly, and what should I know that isn't written down. I'd map the team's decision rights and the metrics and how each is defined, and run one funnel query with the data scientist. &quot;By day 30 I'd share a one-page summary of what I learned and what I still don't understand. I wouldn't propose a large change before I understand the history: a good early contribution is a small thing I own, such as a metric definition or a weekly report.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> A concrete plan; the manager agreement; a day-30 output; restraint.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c37-03\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C37-03</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> interview-craft · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C37 · <strong>Competency:</strong> IC · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> How did you prepare for this interview, and what did you learn about yourself?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I practiced a lot and I feel ready.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> A baseline, a plan by gaps and a result.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Outside scoring, a calibration finding, a written gate, a number for improvement, and a self-knowledge insight that changed a habit.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;I started with a baseline: three recorded mocks scored by an outside scorer, which showed analytics as my weakest area at 2.9 against 3.8 on product sense. I allocated drill hours by importance times gap, so the biggest share went to analytics, and re-scored every two months. After six mocks I computed my bias: my self-scores ran about 0.6 above the outside scorer's, so I corrected for it and used outside scores for decisions. By month ten my analytics average was 3.9 and my bias was under half a point (invented). &quot;What I learned about myself is that I overrate how I sound; the recordings and the outside scores corrected that. I changed one habit as a result: I score before I hear anyone's feedback. I'd bring the same loop to the job: a baseline, outside feedback and small shipped work.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Baseline; gap-driven plan; calibration; evidence of change; a changed habit.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c37-04\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C37-04</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> behavioral · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C37 · <strong>Competency:</strong> BE · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Tell me about a time you taught yourself something quickly.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I learned SQL from videos.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> A goal, a method and a result.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> A baseline, a practice designed around feedback, a result with a number, honesty about what was a sandbox, and how the skill was used.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> <em>(A composite example; figures are invented.)</em> &quot;I couldn't answer my own product questions without asking an analyst, which took two days. I decided to learn SQL well enough to answer funnel, retention and segment questions myself. I built a practice sandbox: a small synthetic dataset in SQLite, and I wrote three queries a week, each with a stated question, the output, one caveat and one decision it would inform. I asked the data scientist on my team to review one query a week. &quot;In six weeks my turnaround for a basic funnel question dropped from two days to under an hour, and in week five I found that one channel brought 35% of our signups with the lowest activation, which changed a test we were planning. I'm clear that the sandbox was practice; the real analysis used our data after the data scientist confirmed my definitions. I still ask for a review on anything that will drive a decision.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> A baseline; a designed practice; a result with a number; honesty about practice versus real work.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c37-05\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C37-05</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> interview-craft · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C37 · <strong>Competency:</strong> IC · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> There is far too much to learn for this role. How do you decide what to learn first?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;The most important things first.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Uses importance and gaps.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Measures the gaps with outside scoring, weights by importance, allocates time proportionally, sets numeric targets, re-measures and reallocates, and keeps a minimum on strengths.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;I measure before I choose. I take outside-scored practice results by competency, weight each by how much it matters for the role, and multiply by the gap to a target level: importance times gap. Then I allocate time in proportion. For example, with analytics at 2.9, AI at 2.6 and problem solving at 4.0, and weights of 3, 2 and 3, the products are 6.3, 4.8 and 3.0, so analytics and AI get most of the hours and problem solving a smaller maintenance share. I set a numeric target and a re-score date for the top two, and reallocate when the gaps change. I keep a minimum on strengths, because they decay without practice.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Measure then weight; proportional allocation; targets and re-scores.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c37-06\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C37-06</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> execution · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C37 · <strong>Competency:</strong> EX · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> What would you ship in your first 90 days, and how would you know it worked?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;A small feature.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> A small change with a metric.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> A small, low-risk change chosen from evidence, a success criterion and review date written before the build, a staged release, and a retrospective.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;I'd pick a small, low-risk change chosen from what I learned in the first 60 days, for example the step with the largest drop in the onboarding funnel. Say it's a clearer explanation on a permissions step, where 38% of new users drop. I'd write the success criterion before building: completion up by at least three points, with support contacts flat as a guardrail, reviewed four weeks after launch. I'd release to 10% of users first with a rollback trigger (C17). &quot;At day 90 I'd review the result against the criterion and write a one-page retrospective: what the metric did, what I'd change, and the plan for the next quarter, agreed with my manager. If it didn't move, I'd say so and say what I learned; a small measured change that failed still builds credibility.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Evidence-chosen change; criterion first; staged release; a retrospective.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c37-07\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C37-07</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> behavioral · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C37 · <strong>Competency:</strong> BE · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Three weeks into the job your manager is very busy and gives you no direction. What do you do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I'd wait for direction,&quot; or &quot;I'd start building something.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Proposes a plan and asks for feedback.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Writes a 30-60-90 draft, makes asking cheap for the manager, uses peers and documents to learn, delivers a small win, and keeps the manager informed on a fixed rhythm.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;I'd make it easy for my manager to give direction. I'd write a draft 30-60-90 plan with what I intend to learn, own and ship, send it with three specific questions, and ask for a 20-minute slot to correct it. Meanwhile I'd learn from other sources: peer product managers, the launch reviews and metrics, the partners' one-to-ones. I'd pick one small thing to own and deliver it, so that I'm useful while direction forms. I'd send a four-line weekly update (done, next, blockers, one ask) so my manager can see progress with a minute's reading, and say plainly when I need a decision. &quot;If after another two weeks there's still no direction, I'd name it directly and kindly: 'I've been working from this plan; is this the right priority, or should I change it?' A manager who is busy usually responds well to a draft they can correct.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> A draft plan; low-cost asks; learning from other sources; a small win; a weekly update.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c37-08\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C37-08</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> behavioral · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C37 · <strong>Competency:</strong> BE · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> After 90 days, how will you know whether you are performing at the level expected of an associate product manager, and what if you are not?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;My manager will tell me.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names indicators and asks for feedback.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Defines indicators with the manager at the start, checks them at 30, 60 and 90 days with partner feedback, compares with evidence (a shipped change, reliable updates), diagnoses gaps early and makes a plan.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;I'd agree indicators with my manager at the start, in writing, so that the standard is shared: for example, that I can explain the product's users, metrics and decisions; that I have shipped one small change with a measure; that partners find me reliable and clear; and that my updates are on time. I'd check them myself at 30, 60 and 90 days against evidence, and ask for feedback at day 45 from my manager and two partners: one thing to keep and one to change. &quot;If I'm behind on one, I'd say so early and make a specific plan: if I haven't shipped, I'd shrink the work; if a partner finds me unreliable, I'd look at dates and scope promises I've made and fix the process. I'd also check my own calibration: if my read of how I'm doing differs from my manager's by more than a little, I'd ask for examples. The aim is to find out at day 45, not at the review.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Agreed indicators; evidence; early feedback; a plan; calibration.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"appendix-f\"></span>\n";
